    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the vector field $F(x,y)?$\n\n- $F(x,y)=⟨0,\\,x⟩$\n- $F(x,y)=⟨4 e^{y},\\,y⟩$\n- $F(x,y)=⟨- \\frac{y}{7} + \\operatorname{atan}{\\left(x \\right)},\\,\\frac{6 x}{7}⟩$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "According to Green's theorem, if $F(x,y)=⟨P(x,y),\\,Q(x,y)⟩,$ where\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field I give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(1\\right)-\\left(0\\right)=\\left(1\\right)-\\left(0\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- Pair II does *not* give the required area. In this case, \n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=4 e^{y}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(0\\right)-\\left(4 e^{y}\\right)=\\left(0\\right)-\\left(4 e^{y}\\right)=- 4 e^{y}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Pair III gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=- \\frac{1}{7}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{6}{7},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(\\frac{6}{7}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{1}{7}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{6}{7}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{1}{7}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "ef76c9e5-4d04-5867-919e-835844b7da7e",
